  "query_preview": "..\nA positively-tilted longwave trough continues to dig southward \ninto the Pacific NW. Ample moisture is advecting from the Pacific\ninto our region via SW mid-level flow aloft. This system is \nalready causing impacts upstream, such as heavy snowfall across \nmany roadways across Billings and Great Falls. Here in Glasgow, \nwe are observing rainfall, but the timing of transition from rain \nto snow is uncertain, lowering confidence in snowfall amounts. It\nlooks to be transitioning earlier than previously forecasted, \nwhich may heighten anticipated snow totals. The rain to snow\nswitch will likely occur at the surface this evening. This shift\ncould occur earlier depending on the strength of the surface \ntrough, which may cause more intense surface cooling.\n\nForecast QPF totals from this system continue to trend higher \ncompared to previous model guidance. Much of our region may see as\nmuch as half an inch to an inch of total precipitation, with \nlower amounts in Wibaux County. Much of the already fallen precipitation\nhas been rain, which will then mix with and change to snow this \nevening, resulting in accumulating snow remaining confined to \nnorthern Valley and northern, southwestern Phillips counties.\n\nWarm ground temperatures, exceeding 40 degrees, will prevent \nsnowfall from sticking to the ground, preventing significant\naccumulation, with the exception of the Little Rockies and higher\nelevations along the Canadian border. As a result, a Winter \nWeather Advisory remains in effect for southwestern Phillips \nCounty for up to 6 inches of snowfall, with higher amounts \npossible depending on elevation. Another Winter Weather Advisory \nhas been issued for northern Phillips and northern Valley \nCounties, beginning 6 PM tonight through noon Monday. \n\nTemperatures for Sunday and Monday will be much cooler than \nthe seasonally typical due to cold polar air lingering along the \nbackside of the cold front. Beyond Monday, temperatures should \ngradually warm up as an upper-level cut-off low develops over the\nFour Corners region and advects warmer air into the CWA.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n\nConfidence in below average temperatures through Monday is high, \nbut decreases as the week progresses, becoming low by Friday. \nPrecipitation chances later in the week depend on the track of \nthe low pressure system over the Four Corners. \n\nModel agreement deteriorates drastically beyond Wednesday; \nconfidence remains low for weather conditions mid-week onward. \nThe GEFS and EPS do not concur about the ejection of the cut-off \nlow. It could eject through the northerly jet or continue \nmeandering over the Four Corners. \n\nConfidence is low regarding QPF, as each model run it has trending\nhigher than the last. Localized areas may receive more than forecast.\n\n\nFIN",
